# Template for .sweep-patterns.local (which is gitignored — NEVER commit it).
#
# One extended regex per line. Blank lines and # comments are ignored.
#   plain line   -> matched case-INSENSITIVE against every tracked file
#   CS: prefix   -> matched case-SENSITIVE (uppercase ticket / project codes;
#                   a blanket case-insensitive match clobbers legitimate
#                   lowercase words that merely share the prefix)
#
# Put the REAL banned strings only in .sweep-patterns.local, so they never
# appear in the repo itself. Everything below is invented.
#
# Typical categories:
#
#   \bacme(corp|vpn)?\b            <- product / company / workspace names
#   \bexample-internal\.com\b      <- internal hostnames and domains
#   \bjdoe\b|\bjanedoe\b           <- real person handles
#   \b(?:10\.13\.)                 <- infrastructure address prefixes, not
#                                     single addresses: an adjacent host in
#                                     a known range walks past a single one
#   CS:\b(ACME|OPS|IOSP)-[0-9]+\b  <- ticket IDs
#   CS:\b(ACME|OPS|IOSP)\b         <- and the BARE codes: a prefix with no
#                                     digit slips past a PREFIX-<digit> rule
#
# Use \b word boundaries rather than lookaround: the same file is matched by
# grep -E over the tree and by perl over history, and only \b works in both.
#
# Note that credential SHAPES (xox…, glpat-…, AKIA…, PEM headers) are built
# into scripts/sweep.sh and need no entry here — a format is safe to publish,
# a value is not.
